{"builder":"Make one pull request that meets both acceptance lines of issue https://github.com/o/settings/issues/31:\n- \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\n- \"npm test passes\"\n\nContext from the repository as it stands:\n- src/config.js: `loadConfig(path)` returns `JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'))`, so bad JSON throws a SyntaxError.\n- src/app.js merges `loadConfig(...)` over `DEFAULTS` inside a try/catch that prints `bad config: <message>` and exits 1.\n- test/config.test.js has a test 'bad JSON throws' that asserts a SyntaxError. That test contradicts the first acceptance line.\n- README.md says an invalid JSON file stops the app with `bad config: <message>` and exit 1.\n- `npm test` runs `node --test`.\n\nSteps:\n1. In src/config.js, read the file with `readFileSync(path, 'utf8')` outside the try block, so read errors such as a missing file still throw as before. Put only `JSON.parse` inside try/catch. On a parse error (a SyntaxError), return `{}`. Rethrow any error that is not a SyntaxError.\n2. The issue's underlying problem is that people can't tell which file is broken or where. Returning `{}` with no message would hide the typo. So in the catch, also write one line to stderr (for example with `process.stderr.write` or `console.error`) that names `path` and includes the parse error's message, which holds the position. Still return `{}`. Do not throw.\n3. In test/config.test.js, replace 'bad JSON throws' with a test that `loadConfig` on a file containing `{port: 9000` returns a value deep-equal to `{}` and does not throw. Keep the 'reads a config' test. You may add a test that a missing file still throws.\n4. Update README.md so it matches the new behaviour. A file that is not valid JSON now gives a warning on stderr naming the file, and the app starts with the defaults. Leave src/app.js's try/catch as it is, because it still handles read errors.\n5. Run `npm test` and confirm every test passes. Then commit, and open the pull request referencing issue #31.","verifier":"Check the pull request head against the two acceptance lines of issue https://github.com/o/settings/issues/31, in this order. Run everything from the repository root at the PR head. Install nothing beyond what the repository needs (it has no dependencies; `npm test` runs `node --test`).\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\nRun this script with Node from the repository root. It writes invalid JSON to a temp file and calls the exported loadConfig:\n```\nnode --input-type=module -e \"import {mkdtempSync,writeFileSync} from 'node:fs';import {tmpdir} from 'node:os';import {join} from 'node:path';import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js';const f=join(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(),'cfg-')),'config.json');writeFileSync(f,'{port: 9000');let r;try{r=loadConfig(f);}catch(e){console.log('THREW',e&&e.name);process.exit(2);}console.log('RESULT',JSON.stringify(r),typeof r,Array.isArray(r));process.exit(JSON.stringify(r)==='{}'&&typeof r==='object'&&r!==null&&!Array.isArray(r)?0:3);\"\n```\nPASS: the script exits 0 and prints `RESULT {} object false`. That means `loadConfig` did not throw on invalid JSON and returned an empty plain object. Also, `loadConfig` is still defined in and exported from src/config.js (the script imports it from there).\nFAIL: the script prints THREW, returns anything other than an empty plain object, exits non-zero, or src/config.js no longer exports `loadConfig`.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"npm test passes\"\nRun `npm test` at the PR head.\nPASS: the command exits 0 and the node test runner reports 0 failed tests.\nFAIL: the command exits non-zero, or any test fails or is cancelled.\n\nAdvisory (does not affect PASS or FAIL):\n- Check that `loadConfig` on a valid file such as `{\"port\": 9000}` still returns `{ port: 9000 }`.\n- Check that a missing file still throws instead of returning `{}`, because only the parse error should be caught.\n- The issue's motivation is that people can't tell which file is broken or where. Note whether a parse failure writes a stderr message naming the path and the error's position, and whether README.md was updated to match the new behaviour.\n- Note whether the old 'bad JSON throws' test was replaced by one asserting `{}`, and not just deleted."}